--TEST--
`lastId` is a reading of the write that produced it and the connection has no member for it at all, so an unrelated statement in between cannot make it stale — `rule:core-classes/db-statement-members` and `rule:core-classes/db-one-api`, spec § 18
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `mysqli_insert_id` and `PDO::lastInsertId` read a *session* value: any
// statement in between replaces it, and the bug that produces is silent. Spec
// § 18 says outright that there is no `lastInsertId` here, and this is what
// stands in its place — each write holds its own answer, and holding it is
// what makes the question unambiguous.
var $db = Core\Db::connect("main");

var $ada = $db->execute("insert into accounts (owner) values (?) returning id", ["ada"]);
var $grace = $db->execute("insert into accounts (owner) values (?) returning id", ["grace"]);

// Each write answers about itself, and the second one arriving does nothing to
// the first: there is no shared place for it to overwrite.
?uint $first = $ada->lastId();
?uint $second = $grace->lastId();
uint $written = $grace->affected();
?uint $reported = $grace->changed();

// And the spelling that has no member behind it, which is § 18's "there is no
// `lastInsertId`" asserted rather than described: the connection never held
// one to go stale.
var $stale = $db->lastId();
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0405]: `Core\Db\Connection` has no method named `lastId`
   --> case.nvs:22:14
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
